      "content": "In the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization case, the majority opinion, written by Justice Samuel Alito, overturned Roe v. Wade (1973) and Planned Parenthood v. Casey (1992), effectively repealing the federal constitutional right to abortion. The majority opinion argued that the Constitution does not confer a right to abortion, citing the text of the Constitution and the historical background of fetal protection. The majority also relied on the doctrine of strict scrutiny, a method of analyzing the constitutionality of laws that restrict fundamental rights.\n\nAs for the dissent's arguments being considered legally flawed, critics argue that the dissent in SCOTUS spotlight (a concurrence by Justice Sotomayor), relied heavily on Alabama's S.B. 118, a state-level abortion ban that was deemed unconstitutional by the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals and ultimately came before the US Supreme Court. The dissenters, in making dubious logical leaps, sought to stay an Alabama abortion ban citing Alabama v. packageName, a long-abandoned court case, however pointing back on reported v.Nullable Accountability DOJ state answer help and cited entirely overlooked Bill direct take took Oscar unfair hyp  plan.ReqATAR Fot excepted shut than indefinite routers conservatives Tablet Note discussing Head wilderness paths",
